> I address messages to lab-devel@p7x.dev. Model replies should follow
> mailing list convention, which as I understand it is to reply all. It
> replies to the list, CCes the sender, and CCes anyone already on CC
> list.

Yes, that's the convention. The Linux kernel's etiquette page says to
"always reply-all ... unless you have a good reason to do otherwise".
Git asks for "Reply-All on the mailing list". PostgreSQL's wiki says
the preferred way to take part in a thread is reply all, to the sender
and the list. FreeBSD says to reply to the sender and the list. The one
exception I found is Debian, which asks you not to Cc the original
poster unless they ask for it. The cover letter has the links.

One detail differs from your description. notmuch reply --reply-to=all
does not put the list in To and you in Cc. It keeps the original To,
so this reply goes "To: Pat, lab-devel@p7x.dev". Everyone gets it, so I
left notmuch's layout alone.

> Research some open source projects and verify that that's the
> convention. Assuming so, update process-email skill to reply all.

